  "enhanced_information": "The patient's symptoms of pruritus, jaundice, and hepatosplenomegaly, combined with a history of ulcerative colitis, strongly suggest primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC). Standard medical consensus indicates that MRCP is the preferred non-invasive diagnostic tool for PSC, as it can effectively visualize bile duct strictures characteristic of the disease. While ERCP can also show bile duct strictures, it is more invasive and typically used for therapeutic purposes rather than initial diagnosis. Liver biopsy can show 'onion-skin' fibrosis, but it is not the first-line diagnostic test for PSC. Ultrasound and contrast CT are less specific for PSC diagnosis. Therefore, MRCP is the most appropriate test to confirm the diagnosis of PSC in this patient.",
  "analysis": "The patient's symptoms of pruritus and jaundice, along with a history of ulcerative colitis and hepatosplenomegaly, suggest primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) as the most likely diagnosis. PSC is commonly associated with ulcerative colitis and can lead to bile duct strictures and liver damage. Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) is a non-invasive imaging technique that is highly effective in visualizing the bile ducts and is commonly used to diagnose PSC by identifying characteristic bile duct changes. While ERCP can also diagnose PSC, MRCP is preferred due to its non-invasive nature.",
